# Notes notebook trash publication proposal

This is a source-only integration map. Production and every frozen stage/review remain unchanged. No test, original helper, host or browser was executed while preparing this map. The parent must install the proposed files, run fresh tests against the installed source, and record actual UI acceptance separately.

`references.json` binds four immutable collections: the original 325-artifact Notes trash stage, prior 37-artifact negative independent review, 504-artifact publication correction, and fresh 61-artifact finite independent review. Their manifests cover 927 artifacts. Each exact manifest and freeze file is also mapped, giving 935 inspected incoming files. `public-preservation-map.json` proposes 920 byte-identical inert files. `local-exclusions.json` excludes fifteen copies of the existing synthetic TAR/JEX fixture by content and hash, plus four generated/private files outside the strict inventories. No whole-tree-public claim is made. All fifteen archive copies have SHA256 `044885c906ba7d8cb97a95fbdd6bc2323ba31a3c301e7ed1d6e592acec8944af`; none is an original executable. No MZ/ELF, ZIP, 7z, GZIP or KDBX carrier was found in the proposed evidence, including recognized JSON base64 values. This bounded content inspection is not a universal secret detector.

Each preservation source path receives an additional `.snapshot` suffix, even when it already has one. This prevents collisions between a live experiment source and an earlier exact snapshot. Any `.local` directory segment is mapped to `private-study-records`; the source path and exact hash remain in the mapping. The public paths are below `apps/notes/results/notebook-trash-followup/{stage,prior-review,publication-fix,publication-review}`. Historical manifest internal paths stay unchanged and are interpreted through the explicit mapping, not silently rebound to public destinations. All inert failed runs, source fingerprints, logs and notices are preserved unless their exact bytes are a classified native archive carrier. The frozen complete inventories remain locally available, including every exclusion.

`live-copy-list.json` proposes thirteen installed files. The corrected client/server and new native notebook-trash runtime are exact. The new integration and DOM tests are exact. Two test-only original oracles adapt only their provenance root, JSON snapshot names, and source-record/special-record `.snapshot` suffix reads. One native test changes only its generated observation output path. The original seven reviewer cases, seven adjacent publication cases, four fresh controls and their DOM fixture change only import/fixture paths and private output defaults. Assertions and original helper semantics stay unchanged. The README proposal changes stage links and adds finite correction/review scope. No shared host, root runner, global metadata or existing historical receipt change is proposed.

The existing installed `fixtures/pinned-joplin.jex` remains a prerequisite of the inherited integration/native tests; it is a separately authenticated synthetic fixture from historical serialization evidence, not a new public preservation carrier. The parent should authenticate its hash before current-layout execution. Normal tests write synthetic details to ignored Studio `.local`, outside `apps/results`, and use owned temporary directories for file-backed data. Node 24 with TypeScript stripping is required for source-sliced helper comparisons. The complete released Joplin application, database and GUI are never run by these helpers. Exact Joplin Desktop 3.6.14 source at commit `7e2765a3857754d12dbadea442b60ceaf9d4b23d`, AGPL-3.0-or-later notices and source provenance remain preserved. The separate older renderer serialization study and its 22 checks stay separate.

The correction's 55 author tests and independent 18 tests are separate historical campaigns. The proposed current-layout total of 73 is prospective and has not been run in this plan. The independent eighteen comprise seven old reviewer bodies, seven author adjacent bodies and four fresh controls; all passed with zero skips and stable sources. Its one new authenticated original helper context/top-level restore matched eleven item projections and 55 scalar fields through nine exact slices using owned memory/SQL adapters and a fixed clock. Original application/database/GUI executions are zero. Prior original/helper counts remain separate.

Actual browser acceptance is incomplete in the independent receipt. The exact corrected client opened an owned note and an actual completed HTTP Save was held after revision 2 reached disk. A native JavaScript trash confirmation appeared, but browser bridge dialog/focus commands stalled; no notebook-trash request reached the backend. Tool failures are labeled transcriptions, not application faults or raw child logs. The owned host/relay exited normally and its temporary directory was removed; owned Chrome tab closure remains unconfirmed. The parent plans a separate actual UI campaign, potentially through the in-app browser, whose native confirmation handling may differ.

`registry-identity-binding.json` observes the current external parent registries' 159 source fingerprints and 232 stable capability IDs without copying or editing them. Older registry/provenance bindings remain separately preserved. These are capability areas, not an exhaustive atomic-feature ledger or completed-product claim. Native limits, earlier lifecycle/input-ownership gaps, synchronization/security/complete product parity and superiority remain open as documented.

The failed first map assembly is retained under `failures/collision-v1`: it stopped before writing publication records because a raw source and its historical snapshot collided. The consistent extra suffix repaired only this map construction. No runtime implementation failure is inferred.
